% ============================ ABSTRACT (page 2) ============================
\section*{Abstract}
\addcontentsline{toc}{section}{Abstract}
Copper is the cornerstone of the Zambian economy, consistently
accounting for the bulk of national export earnings, so the statistical behaviour of its
price is of direct fiscal and commercial interest. This study models
\Sexpr{format(nrow(cu),big.mark=",")} daily copper prices (US\$/tonne) spanning
\Sexpr{format(min(cu$Date),"%b %Y")}--\Sexpr{format(max(cu$Date),"%b %Y")}. The price level is
non-stationary, so the analysis works with daily log-returns. Following the Box--Jenkins
methodology, the autocorrelation and partial-autocorrelation functions together with the
Bayesian Information Criterion identify an \textbf{AR(2)} model for the conditional mean.
The fitted ARMA(2,0) removes the linear dependence in returns, but its residuals display
strong volatility clustering and heavy tails, and out of sample it does not outperform a
naive random walk. The study therefore extends the specification to an
\textbf{ARMA(2,0)--GARCH(1,1)} model with Student-$t$ innovations. The extension lowers the
BIC from \Sexpr{format(round(BIC(fit)),big.mark=",")} to
\Sexpr{format(round(BICg),big.mark=",")}, eliminates the autoregressive conditional
heteroskedasticity from the standardised residuals, and captures both the persistence of
volatility ($\alpha_1+\beta_1=\Sexpr{round(pers,3)}$) and the fat tails
($t$ d.f.\ $\approx\Sexpr{round(tdf,1)}$) characteristic of commodity returns. The results
imply that copper prices are close to weak-form efficient in the mean, while their
\emph{risk} is highly predictable --- the practically useful object to forecast.

