% ============================ CHAPTER 1 ============================
\section{Introduction}

\subsection{Background}
Copper is the single most important commodity in the Zambian economy, routinely
contributing the majority of the country's foreign-exchange earnings and a substantial
share of government revenue (World Bank, 2023). Because the national budget, the exchange
rate and the mining sector's investment decisions are all sensitive to the copper price,
understanding and forecasting that price is of considerable practical importance.

Commodity prices such as copper are typically studied as financial time series. The seminal
framework is that of Box and Jenkins (1976), who formalised the autoregressive
moving-average (ARMA) class of models and an iterative \emph{identification--estimation--%
diagnostic-checking} procedure for fitting them. A recurring empirical finding for prices of
metals, equities and currencies is that, although the \emph{level} of a price is
non-stationary and behaves much like a random walk, its \emph{volatility} is highly
predictable: large changes tend to be followed by large changes and small by small. This
``volatility clustering'' motivated the autoregressive conditional heteroskedasticity (ARCH)
model of Engle (1982) and its generalisation, the GARCH model of Bollerslev (1986).

\subsection{Statement of the Problem}
A classical ARMA model assumes that the error variance is constant over time
(homoskedasticity) and, for interval forecasts, that the errors are normally distributed.
Daily commodity returns routinely violate both assumptions. If an analyst fits an ARMA model
to copper returns and stops there, the point estimates may be reasonable but the standard
errors, confidence intervals and any value-at-risk figures derived from the model will be
unreliable, because the model has ignored the time-varying risk that dominates the data.
This study asks two questions: \emph{(i)} what ARMA model best describes the conditional mean
of daily copper returns, and \emph{(ii)} does explicitly modelling the conditional variance
with a GARCH component materially improve the model?

\subsection{Aim and Objectives}
The aim is to build, validate and improve a time-series model for daily copper prices. The
specific objectives are:
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item to transform the price series to a stationary form suitable for ARMA modelling;
  \item to identify and estimate an ARMA model for the conditional mean using the ACF, PACF
        and the Bayesian Information Criterion (BIC);
  \item to assess the adequacy of that model through residual diagnostics and out-of-sample
        forecast accuracy; and
  \item to extend the model with a GARCH component and quantify the resulting improvement.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Scope and Limitations}
The study is univariate: it uses only the history of the copper price itself and does not
incorporate macroeconomic or market covariates such as the US dollar index, Chinese
industrial demand or inventory levels. The data are daily spot prices in US dollars per
tonne; exchange-rate effects on the kwacha value of copper are outside the scope. The
analysis is conducted entirely in R, ensuring full reproducibility.

\subsection{Organisation of the Study}
Section~2 describes the data and the methodology. Section~3 presents the ARMA modelling and
its diagnostics --- the original analysis. Section~4 develops the GARCH extension and
explains why it constitutes an improvement. Section~5 concludes. Full R output is reproduced
in the appendix, and the complete code and data are available in the project repository.

% ============================ CHAPTER 2 ============================
\section{Data and Methodology}

\subsection{Data}
The dataset comprises \Sexpr{format(n_raw,big.mark=",")} daily observations of the copper
price in US dollars per tonne. The magnitude and trajectory of the series (rising from
roughly US\$1,800/tonne in 2000 to a peak above US\$10,800/tonne) are consistent with London
Metal Exchange cash-settlement quotations. Three data-quality issues were corrected before
analysis: \Sexpr{n_typo} dates had been keyed with the year 19xx instead of 20xx (the entire
January--February 2022 block), which were shifted forward by one century; \Sexpr{n_dup}
duplicate date was removed; and the series was sorted chronologically. The cleaned series
contains \Sexpr{format(nrow(cu),big.mark=",")} observations spanning
\Sexpr{format(min(cu$Date),"%d %B %Y")} to \Sexpr{format(max(cu$Date),"%d %B %Y")}.

\subsection{Returns and Stationarity}
Let $P_t$ denote the closing price on day $t$. The price level trends strongly and is
non-stationary, so the analysis uses the continuously-compounded (log) return, expressed in
percent,
\begin{equation}
  r_t = 100\times\ln\!\left(\frac{P_t}{P_{t-1}}\right).
\end{equation}
Stationarity is assessed with the Augmented Dickey--Fuller (ADF) test of Dickey and Fuller
(1979), whose null hypothesis is a unit root, and the KPSS test of Kwiatkowski et al.\ (1992),
whose null is stationarity.

\subsection{The ARMA Model}
An ARMA$(p,q)$ model for the return is
\begin{equation}
  r_t = \mu + \sum_{i=1}^{p}\phi_i\,r_{t-i} + \varepsilon_t + \sum_{j=1}^{q}\theta_j\,\varepsilon_{t-j},
  \qquad \varepsilon_t \sim \text{i.i.d.}(0,\sigma^2).
\end{equation}
Candidate orders are read from the sample autocorrelation function (ACF) and partial
autocorrelation function (PACF): an AR($p$) process produces a PACF that cuts off after lag
$p$, and an MA($q$) process an ACF that cuts off after lag $q$ (Box \& Jenkins, 1976). Among
competing specifications the preferred model minimises the Bayesian Information Criterion,
$\text{BIC}=-2\ell+k\ln n$ (Schwarz, 1978), where $\ell$ is the maximised log-likelihood,
$k$ the number of parameters and $n$ the sample size. The BIC is reported alongside Akaike's
AIC (Akaike, 1974); BIC is preferred here because it penalises complexity more heavily and is
consistent for the true order in large samples.

\subsection{Diagnostics and Out-of-Sample Evaluation}
An adequate model should leave white-noise residuals. This is tested with the Ljung--Box
$Q$-statistic (Ljung \& Box, 1978) on the residuals and on the squared residuals, the latter
detecting ARCH effects; the ARCH-LM test of Engle (1982) provides a second check, and the
Jarque--Bera test (Jarque \& Bera, 1987) examines normality. Forecast performance is judged
out of sample: the model is estimated on all but the final \Sexpr{H} trading days and used to
produce one-step-ahead forecasts over that hold-out window, which are compared against a
naive random-walk benchmark ($\hat P_t = P_{t-1}$) using the root-mean-square error (RMSE)
and mean absolute error (MAE).

\subsection{The GARCH Extension}
To capture volatility clustering, the constant-variance assumption is relaxed by letting the
conditional variance $\sigma_t^2$ follow a GARCH(1,1) process (Bollerslev, 1986):
\begin{equation}
  \sigma_t^2 = \omega + \alpha_1\,\varepsilon_{t-1}^2 + \beta_1\,\sigma_{t-1}^2,
  \qquad \omega>0,\;\alpha_1,\beta_1\ge 0,\;\alpha_1+\beta_1<1,
\end{equation}
with the AR(2) mean retained from Section~3. The quantity $\alpha_1+\beta_1$ measures the
persistence of volatility shocks. To accommodate the heavy tails of commodity returns, the
innovations are assumed to follow a standardised Student-$t$ distribution, whose
degrees-of-freedom parameter is estimated jointly with the model.

\subsection{Software}
All computation uses R: \texttt{readxl} for data import, \texttt{tseries} for the unit-root
and normality tests, \texttt{forecast} for ARMA estimation and forecasting, \texttt{lmtest}
and \texttt{FinTS} for diagnostics, and \texttt{rugarch} for the GARCH model.

% ============================ CHAPTER 3 ============================
\section{Results: The ARMA Model}

\subsection{Price Behaviour and Returns}
Figure~\ref{fig:level} plots the price level and its logarithm. The series wanders without
reverting to a fixed mean --- the visual signature of non-stationarity --- with the
2008 commodity crash, the 2011 and 2021--2024 booms all clearly visible. Figure~\ref{fig:ret}
shows the log-returns, which fluctuate around zero but in distinct calm and turbulent
episodes: the hallmark of volatility clustering that motivates Section~4.

<<fig-level, fig.cap='Daily copper price (top) and log price (bottom). The wandering, non-mean-reverting level is characteristic of a non-stationary, near-random-walk series.\\label{fig:level}', fig.height=4.2>>=
par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(2.4,4,1.8,1), cex=0.8)
plot(cu$Date, price, type="l", col="#c2410c", lwd=1,
     main="Daily copper price (USD/tonne)", xlab="", ylab="USD/tonne")
plot(cu$Date, log(price), type="l", col="#1b3a2d", lwd=1,
     main="Log price", xlab="", ylab="log(USD/tonne)")
@

<<fig-ret, fig.cap='Daily log-returns (\\%). Volatility arrives in clusters --- calm stretches punctuated by turbulent ones --- which a constant-variance ARMA model cannot represent.\\label{fig:ret}', fig.height=2.6>>=
par(mar=c(2.4,4,1.2,1), cex=0.8)
plot(cu$Date[-1], ret, type="l", col="#c2410c", lwd=0.5, xlab="", ylab="% return")
abline(h=0, col="grey60")
@

\subsection{Stationarity Tests}
The tests confirm the visual impression. On the price level the ADF test fails to reject a
unit root ($p=\Sexpr{round(adf_lvl$p.value,3)}$) while KPSS rejects stationarity
($p\le\Sexpr{round(kpss_lvl$p.value,3)}$). On the returns the verdict reverses: ADF strongly
rejects a unit root ($p\le\Sexpr{round(adf_ret$p.value,3)}$) and KPSS does not reject
stationarity ($p\ge\Sexpr{round(kpss_ret$p.value,2)}$). The returns are therefore stationary
and suitable for ARMA modelling; equivalently, the log price is integrated of order one,
$I(1)$.

\subsection{Order Selection: ACF, PACF and BIC}
The ACF and PACF of the returns (Figure~\ref{fig:acf}) both show significant spikes at lags 1
and 2 that then die away --- a pattern consistent with a low-order autoregression. The BIC
grid search over $p,q\in\{0,\dots,5\}$ confirms this: the minimum BIC is attained by the
AR(2) specification, ARMA(2,0). The eight best models are listed below (R output).

<<fig-acf, fig.cap='ACF and PACF of daily log-returns. Significant spikes at lags 1--2 in both functions point to a low-order AR process.\\label{fig:acf}', fig.height=4.0>>=
par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(3.4,4,2,1), cex=0.8)
Acf(ret, lag.max=30, main="ACF of log-returns")
Pacf(ret, lag.max=30, main="PACF of log-returns")
@

<<bic-grid, echo=TRUE>>=
head(gres[order(gres$BIC), ], 8)   # candidate ARMA orders ranked by BIC (lowest = best)
@

\subsection{The Fitted ARMA(2,0) Model}
Estimating the selected model gives the coefficients below. Both autoregressive terms are
highly significant ($p<10^{-9}$); the constant (mean daily return) is not significantly
different from zero, consistent with prices that drift only weakly.

<<arma-fit, echo=TRUE>>=
fit <- Arima(ret, order = c(2,0,0), include.mean = TRUE)
coeftest(fit)
@

\subsection{Residual Diagnostics}
The model captures the \emph{linear} dependence in returns: the Ljung--Box test on the
residuals is insignificant at the 5\% level ($Q_{20}$ $p=\Sexpr{round(lb_r$p.value,3)}$), so
no autocorrelation remains in the mean. However, two assumptions fail decisively. The
Ljung--Box test on the \emph{squared} residuals and the ARCH-LM test both reject the null of
no conditional heteroskedasticity at any conventional level
($p<10^{-10}$), and the
Jarque--Bera test rejects normality. In words, the ARMA residuals are uncorrelated but
\emph{not} independent --- their variance is strongly predictable and their distribution is
heavy-tailed (Figure~\ref{fig:resid}).

<<resid-diag, echo=TRUE>>=
Box.test(residuals(fit), lag = 20, type = "Ljung-Box", fitdf = 2)  # autocorrelation in mean
Box.test(residuals(fit)^2, lag = 20, type = "Ljung-Box")           # ARCH effects (squared)
ArchTest(residuals(fit), lags = 12)                                # Engle's ARCH-LM test
@

<<fig-resid, fig.cap='ARMA(2,0) residuals. The squared-residual ACF (bottom) shows large, slowly-decaying autocorrelation --- undeniable ARCH that the homoskedastic model ignores.\\label{fig:resid}', fig.height=4.0>>=
par(mfrow=c(2,1), mar=c(3.4,4,2,1), cex=0.8)
plot(cu$Date[-1], residuals(fit), type="l", col="#c2410c", lwd=0.4,
     main="ARMA(2,0) residuals", xlab="", ylab="resid")
Acf(residuals(fit)^2, lag.max=30, main="ACF of squared residuals (ARCH signature)")
@

\subsection{Out-of-Sample Forecast Accuracy}
The model was re-estimated on all but the final \Sexpr{H} trading days and used to produce
one-step-ahead forecasts over that window. The point-forecast accuracy is essentially
identical to --- and marginally worse than --- a naive random walk, and the direction of the
next day's move is called correctly only \Sexpr{round(dir_acc,1)}\% of the time
(no better than a coin toss). This is the expected result for a near-efficient price: the
small but statistically significant AR(2) structure is \emph{not} economically exploitable
for point forecasting.

<<bt-table, results='asis'>>=
library(knitr)
print(kable(bt, format="latex", booktabs=TRUE, digits=2,
            caption="Out-of-sample one-step forecast accuracy over the final 252 trading days (price level, USD/tonne). Lower is better.",
            col.names=c("Model","RMSE","MAE")))
@

<<fig-bt, fig.cap='Actual vs.\\ one-step ARMA forecast over the hold-out window. The forecast tracks the lagged price so closely that it is visually indistinguishable from a random walk.\\label{fig:bt}', fig.height=2.7>>=
par(mar=c(3,4,1.4,1), cex=0.8)
d <- cu$Date[te+1]
plot(d, p_act, type="l", col="#1b3a2d", lwd=1.2, xlab="", ylab="USD/tonne",
     main="Hold-out: actual vs ARMA(2,0) one-step forecast")
lines(d, p_arma, col="#c2410c", lwd=1, lty=2)
legend("topleft", c("Actual","ARMA(2,0) forecast"), col=c("#1b3a2d","#c2410c"),
       lty=c(1,2), lwd=c(1.2,1), bty="n", cex=0.85)
@

% ============================ CHAPTER 4 ============================
\section{The Improved Model: ARMA--GARCH}

\subsection{Motivation}
Section~3.5 established that the ARMA(2,0) residuals violate the constant-variance and
normality assumptions: there is pronounced volatility clustering and the tails are far
heavier than Gaussian. Ignoring this has real consequences. The standard errors in the ARMA
output assume homoskedasticity, so the reported significance and any confidence or
value-at-risk intervals are distorted; and a Gaussian interval forecast will be far too wide
in calm periods and far too narrow in turbulent ones. The remedy is to model the conditional
variance explicitly. The AR(2) mean is retained and a GARCH(1,1) variance with Student-$t$
innovations is added.

\subsection{The Fitted ARMA(2,0)--GARCH(1,1) Model}
The estimated parameters are shown below (R output from \texttt{rugarch}). All variance
parameters are highly significant. Two features stand out. First, volatility is highly
persistent: $\alpha_1+\beta_1=\Sexpr{round(pers,3)}$, close to one, meaning shocks to
volatility decay only slowly. Second, the estimated Student-$t$ degrees of freedom is
$\approx\Sexpr{round(tdf,1)}$ --- well below the $\sim$30 at which the $t$ becomes
effectively normal --- formally confirming heavy tails. Notably, once the variance is modelled
the second AR term shrinks toward insignificance, indicating that part of the apparent
mean-dependence in Section~3 was in fact unmodelled heteroskedasticity.

<<garch-fit, echo=TRUE>>=
spec <- ugarchspec(variance.model = list(model = "sGARCH", garchOrder = c(1,1)),
                   mean.model     = list(armaOrder = c(2,0), include.mean = TRUE),
                   distribution.model = "std")            # Student-t innovations
fg <- ugarchfit(spec, data = ret, solver = "hybrid")
round(fg@fit$matcoef, 6)
@

\subsection{Model Comparison}
Table~\ref{tab:cmp} places the two models side by side. The GARCH extension raises the
log-likelihood from \Sexpr{format(round(as.numeric(logLik(fit))),big.mark=",")} to
\Sexpr{format(round(LLg),big.mark=",")} and, despite its three extra parameters, lowers the
BIC by almost \Sexpr{format(round(BIC(fit)-BICg, -2),big.mark=",")} points --- an overwhelming
margin (a BIC difference above 10 is regarded as decisive). Crucially, the diagnostic
columns flip from rejection to non-rejection: the ARCH effects and squared-residual
autocorrelation that contaminated the ARMA residuals are absent from the GARCH
\emph{standardised} residuals.

<<cmp-table, results='asis'>>=
ct <- cmp
ct$logLik <- round(ct$logLik); ct$AIC <- round(ct$AIC); ct$BIC <- round(ct$BIC)
ct$LB_z2_p <- formatC(ct$LB_z2_p, format="g", digits=2)
ct$ARCH_p  <- formatC(ct$ARCH_p,  format="g", digits=2)
print(kable(ct, format="latex", booktabs=TRUE,
            caption="\\label{tab:cmp}ARMA vs.\\ ARMA-GARCH. The GARCH model wins decisively on fit (logLik, AIC, BIC) and is the only one whose (standardised) residuals pass the no-ARCH tests.",
            col.names=c("Model","k","logLik","AIC","BIC","LB($z^2$) p","ARCH-LM p"),
            align=c("l","c","r","r","r","r","r")))
@

\subsection{What the GARCH Model Reveals}
Figure~\ref{fig:vol} plots the estimated conditional volatility $\sigma_t$. Far from being
constant (the flat line the ARMA model implicitly assumes), daily volatility ranges from
under 1\% in calm periods to over 4\% during the 2008 crisis and the 2020 and 2022 shocks.
This time-varying risk is the single most important feature of the data, and it is invisible
to the ARMA model.

<<fig-vol, fig.cap='GARCH(1,1) conditional volatility (annualised-scale daily \\%). Risk is anything but constant; the ARMA model assumes the flat dashed line.\\label{fig:vol}', fig.height=2.7>>=
par(mar=c(3,4,1.4,1), cex=0.8)
plot(cu$Date[-1], cv, type="l", col="#c2410c", lwd=0.7, xlab="", ylab="cond. SD (%/day)",
     main="Estimated conditional volatility")
abline(h=sd(ret), col="#1b3a2d", lty=2)
legend("topright", c("GARCH conditional SD","constant SD (ARMA assumption)"),
       col=c("#c2410c","#1b3a2d"), lty=c(1,2), lwd=c(0.9,1), bty="n", cex=0.8)
@

Figure~\ref{fig:zdiag} confirms the model is now adequate. The ACF of the squared
standardised residuals is flat --- the ARCH has been absorbed --- and the QQ-plot against the
Student-$t$ shows the heavy tails are well described once the right innovation distribution is
used.

<<fig-zdiag, fig.cap='Standardised-residual diagnostics. Left: squared standardised residuals show no remaining ARCH (contrast Figure~4). Right: QQ-plot against the fitted Student-$t$.\\label{fig:zdiag}', fig.height=2.9>>=
par(mfrow=c(1,2), mar=c(3.6,3.8,2,1), cex=0.8)
Acf(z^2, lag.max=30, main="ACF of squared std. residuals")
qd <- qt(ppoints(length(z)), df=tdf)/sqrt(tdf/(tdf-2))
qqplot(qd, sort(z), pch=20, cex=0.3, col="#c2410c",
       main="QQ vs Student-t", xlab="theoretical t quantile", ylab="std. residual")
abline(0,1,col="#1b3a2d")
@

\subsection{Why It Is an Improvement}
The GARCH extension improves on the pure ARMA model in four concrete ways.
\begin{enumerate}[nosep,leftmargin=1.4em]
  \item \textbf{Valid inference.} By modelling the heteroskedasticity rather than ignoring it,
        the parameter standard errors become reliable, so statements about which terms matter
        are trustworthy.
  \item \textbf{Honest, time-varying uncertainty.} The ARMA model attaches the \emph{same}
        forecast interval to every day; the GARCH model widens its intervals in turbulent
        regimes and narrows them in calm ones, matching how copper risk actually behaves.
  \item \textbf{Better density and risk forecasts.} Because it captures volatility clustering
        and fat tails, the model produces accurate value-at-risk and other tail-risk measures
        --- exactly what a treasury or mining-finance desk needs --- where the Gaussian ARMA
        would understate the probability of large losses.
  \item \textbf{Decisive statistical superiority.} It is preferred by both AIC and BIC by a
        wide margin and is the only specification whose residuals pass every diagnostic.
\end{enumerate}
A caveat preserves the honesty of the analysis: the GARCH layer does \emph{not} make the
copper price more predictable \emph{in the mean} --- the point forecast remains close to a
random walk, as Section~3.6 showed. What it makes predictable, and predictable well, is the
\emph{risk}. For copper, that is the quantity worth forecasting.

% ============================ CHAPTER 5 ============================
\section{Conclusions and Recommendations}

\subsection{Summary of Findings}
Daily copper prices over 2000--2025 are non-stationary and behave, in their level, much like a
random walk. The log-returns are stationary, and the ACF, PACF and BIC jointly select an
AR(2) model for the conditional mean. That model is statistically valid in the mean but
inadequate overall: its residuals exhibit strong volatility clustering and heavy tails, and
out of sample it does not beat a naive random walk. Extending it to an
ARMA(2,0)--GARCH(1,1) model with Student-$t$ innovations resolves these defects, with a far
higher likelihood, decisively lower information criteria, clean standardised residuals,
volatility persistence of \Sexpr{round(pers,3)} and tail thickness corresponding to about
\Sexpr{round(tdf,1)} degrees of freedom.

\subsection{Conclusions}
The copper price is approximately weak-form efficient in the mean: past prices carry little
exploitable information about the \emph{direction} of future prices. Its variance, by
contrast, is strongly and persistently predictable. Any serious model of copper-price
behaviour must therefore model the conditional variance; an ARMA model alone is incomplete.

\subsection{Recommendations}
For analysts and risk managers, copper-price risk should be modelled with a GARCH-type
specification and a heavy-tailed innovation distribution, and value-at-risk should be computed
from the conditional rather than the unconditional variance. For forecasting the price level
itself, a random walk (optionally with drift) is a hard-to-beat benchmark, so resources are
better spent on volatility and scenario analysis than on chasing point forecasts. For
policymakers reliant on copper revenue, the persistence of volatility implies that price
shocks are protracted, strengthening the case for stabilisation buffers.

\subsection{Areas for Further Research}
Natural extensions include asymmetric volatility models (EGARCH, GJR-GARCH) to test whether
price drops raise volatility more than equivalent rises; multivariate or ARIMAX models that
bring in the US dollar, Chinese demand and inventory data; regime-switching models for the
distinct boom and crash phases visible in Figure~\ref{fig:level}; and longer-horizon density
forecasts for budgeting applications.

